            'FIXED 2026-09-22: a cycle date is the end of a period, and the shipped packs asked for two that were not. The semimonthly cut said the 1st, which matches no period end lib/periods defines \u2014 it asked for an invoice on the first day of the half-month it would bill, a fortnight before those hours existed, and the 1st-to-15th period was never closed: 20 dates a year with gaps of 1, 3 and 32 days where the 15th and month-end give 24 with gaps of 14 to 18. And the weekly timesheet approval was a Monday series anchored separately at the contract start, so a contract starting Saturday, Sunday or Monday got an approval four days before its first submission \u2014 never claimed by pickCycle, and called overdue on the placement timeline for the life of the contract. It is three days after the hours are due, said with offsetDays, which also gives the last week of a contract the approval date a Monday-anchored series stopped short of. Diagnosed with numbers by etyme-money in lib/cycle-generator, changed in lib/template-packs by etyme-regulatory, and the UK pack carried the identical approval line and was changed with it. On a world seeded afterwards: 0 of 916 adjacent invoice and vendor-bill cycle pairs land within two days of each other, and 0 of 32 placements with hours cycles have an approval before a submission.',
